


End-to-End Steps to Build Chat-Driven Auto-Query App
Step 1: Set Up Your Database and Schema
Prepare your orphanage database with all required tables and columns (e.g., orphans with age, name, etc.).

Keep database schema metadata accessible for your app to validate queries dynamically.

Step 2: Build the Frontend Chat UI
Use React with TypeScript (or any UI framework you prefer).

Implement a chat interface where users input natural language queries.

When the user submits, send the prompt to your backend via HTTP POST.

Step 3: Create Backend Service (No Manual API)
Use .NET Core Web API or Node.js Express as backend.

Implement a single endpoint /chat-query receiving the chat prompt and user context (like logged-in user roles).

Step 4: Integrate NLP/LLM to Parse Prompt and Generate Query
Integrate an LLM service like OpenAI GPT:

Send user prompt + schema metadata + role information as context.

Ask LLM to generate a SQL query or structured data request based on prompt.

Example prompt for LLM:
“Generate a SQL query to list orphans where age between 5 and 6. Allowed tables: Orphans. User role: admin.”

Step 5: Validate Generated Query
Parse the SQL returned by the LLM.

Validate against your database schema and user permissions.

Use a whitelist approach: only allow queries touching permitted tables and columns.

Reject any unsafe or disallowed queries.

Step 6: Execute Query Securely
If validation passes, execute the query safely using parameterized execution on your database.

Catch any errors and send a user-friendly message back to frontend.

Step 7: Format Result for Frontend Display
Convert query result into JSON.

Format result to a UI component model (list, table, cards).

Return this data in the API response.

Step 8: Render the Response on Chat UI
Chat UI receives formatted JSON data.

Dynamically render UI components based on response type.

e.g., a card list of kids with name, age, photo, etc.

Step 9: Logging and Monitoring
Log each request, generated query, executed records, and user ID for auditing.

Monitor and review logs for suspicious activities